ws/the-birdcage/THE-BIRDCAGE-HERO-IMAGE.jpg?auto=compress&crop=focalpoint&fit=crop&fp-x=0.3624&fp-y=0.2679&h=1080&q=80&w=1920","altText":"THE BIRDCAGE HERO IMAGE"},"portraitHeroImage":{"uri":"https://img-assets.drafthouse.com/images/shows/the-birdcage/THE-BIRDCAGE_portrait.jpg?auto=compress&crop=focalpoint&fit=crop&fp-x=0.5&fp-y=0.5&h=1440&q=80&w=1080","altText":"THE BIRDCAGE portrait"},"posterImages":[{"uri":"https://img-assets.drafthouse.com/images/shows/the-birdcage/THE-BIRDCAGE_sp-poster.jpg?auto=compress&fit=clip&h=1620&q=80&w=1080","altText":"THE BIRDCAGE"}],"certification":"R"},"event":{"slug":"queer-film-theory-101-the-birdcage","title":"<show-title>The Birdcage</show-title>","eventTypeSlug":"special-event","eventTypeTitleOverride":null,"headline":"Williams & Lane burst open THE BIRDCAGE in this flamboyant farcical adaptation","description":"<p><em>\"My mother always said, 'Live on Fisher Island, get buried in Palm Beach. That way, you'll have the best of Florida.'\"</em></p>\n<p>Progress is not a straight line. I mean, I’m not sure it’s a line at all. It seems far more abstract and subjective. Like, listen: in 1996, when THE BIRDCAGE was first released, the idea of a mainstream comedy film with two gay leads (and many more openly queer characters) was controversial. I guess, unfortunately…it still is. </p>\n<p>Based on the French farce film LA CAGE AUX FOLLES from 1978, Mike Nichols and Elaine May’s adaptation shifts the action from a drag club in the French Riviera to a drag club in South Beach, Florida…the American Riviera. When Armand Goldman (Robin Williams) learns that his son Val (Dan Futterman) is engaged to the daughter of conservative couple Senator Kevin Keeley (Gene Hackman) and Louise Keeley (Dianne Wiest), he’s put into an impossible situation. Pushed by Val to “straighten” out his life in preparation for meeting his fiancée Barbara’s (Calista Flockhart) parents, Armand struggles to manage his life partner Albert (Nathan Lane) with some dubious assistance from their housemaid Agador (Hank Azaria) and Val’s birth mother Katharine (Christine Brianski). Shenanigans ensue: there’s drag, Fosse, pierced toast, beckoning schmecken, and of course DRAG! WHEW! Grounded by hilarious and deeply heartfelt performances from Williams and Lane, THE BIRDCAGE is an incredible (if sometimes problematic) comedy helmed by the iconic duo of Nichols and May. Immensely quotable and uncomfortably relevant 30 years after its release, THE BIRDCAGE is a classic, and we’re so excited to screen it as part of Queer Film Theory 101!</p>","vistaHoCodes":["A000036788"],"cinemaSessionIds":[],"landscapeHeroImage":null,"portraitHeroImage":null,"trailer":null,"posterImage":null,"additionalStillImages":[],"showSlug":"the-birdcage","brandPriority":"BEST","certification":"R","runtimeMinutes":122,"includeShowDescription":false,"useDeluxeDescription":false,"contentBlocks":[]},"eventType":{"id":"1869","slug":"special-event","title":"Special Event","headline":"For movie lovers, by movie lovers","description":"<p>Whether it’s a movie-inspired feast, an interactive party, or a screening paired with a live performance, we love sharing the movies we love in new and exciting ways.</p>","titleTreatmentImage":{"uri":"https://img-assets.drafthouse.com/images/categories/special-event/SpecialEvent_2020.png?auto=compress&fit=clip&q=80","altText":"Special Event 2020"},"heroImage":{"uri":"https://img-assets.drafthouse.com/images/categories/special-event/EventCategory_Web_Hero_SpecialEvent_1920x1080.png?auto=compress&crop=focalpoint&fit=crop&fp-x=0.5&fp-y=0.5&h=1080&q=80&w=1920","altText":"Special Event"},"titleCardImage":{"uri":"https://img-assets.drafthouse.com/images/categories/special-event/EventCategory_Web_Hero_SpecialEvent_1920x1080_2026-02-10-153941_ieqb.png?auto=compress&crop=focalpoint&fit=crop&fp-x=0.5&fp-y=0.5&h=1080&q=80&w=1920","altText":"Event Category Web Hero Special Event 1920x1080"},"collectionSlug":"special-event"},"formatSlugs":["2d-digital"]